ds:concept.Understanding-the-modifier-contract a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Understanding the modifier contract";
    ds:summary "This document covers what modifiers are and the contract between the modifier side and the component side: the problem modifiers solve, the modifier families, how nesting and overrides behave, where modifiers sit in the cascade, and what modifiers deliberately do not do.";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "**Status:** Final  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Last updated:** 2026-09-11  \n**Source:** `@canonical/react-tokens` Storybook guide — _Understanding the Modifier Contract_\n\n\nModifiers are the mechanism through which a parent container changes the visual output of every descendant component — without those components knowing about the modifier, and without any prop drilling or runtime logic.\n\nThis page explains the contract between modifier classes and component styles.\n\n## The problem modifiers solve\n\nA form has an error. The form's border should turn red, but so should the label text, the input border, the icon color, and the helper text — every piece of the form's visual output should shift to communicate the error state.\n\nWithout modifiers, you would need to thread an `intent=\"error\"` prop into every child component. This couples components to specific contexts, requires runtime coordination, and makes composition fragile. If you add a new child to the form, you must remember to pass the prop.\n\nModifiers solve this by letting a single ancestor class change every descendant's output simultaneously through the CSS cascade. No prop drilling, no React context, no JavaScript at all.\n\n## How the contract works\n\nThe modifier contract has two sides: the **modifier class** sets channel variables, and **component styles** read them through fallback chains.\n\n### The modifier side\n\nWhen an ancestor element applies a modifier class like `.error`, it sets a group of **channel variables**:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.error {\n  --modifier-color-text: var(--color-text-error);\n  --modifier-color-border: var(--color-border-error);\n  --modifier-color-icon: var(--color-icon-error);\n  --modifier-color-foreground-input: var(--color-foreground-input-error);\n  --modifier-color-focus-ring: var(--color-focus-ring-error);\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nThese channel variables are prefixed with `--modifier-` to make their role explicit. They are intermediate — set by modifiers, read by components — and they point to intent-specific semantic tokens (`--color-text-error`, `--color-border-error`).\n\n### The component side\n\nComponents read the channel variable first, with a fallback to the base semantic token:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.button {\n  color: var(--modifier-color-text, var(--color-text));\n  border-color: var(--modifier-color-border, var(--color-border));\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nOutside any modifier context, `--modifier-color-text` is unset, and the component falls through to `--color-text`. Inside `.error`, the channel variable resolves to the error text color. Inside `.success`, it resolves to the success text color. The component code is identical in every case.\n\n## Modifier families\n\nThe system defines several modifier families, each setting the same channel variables to different intent-specific values:\n\n| Family | Classes | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|---------|\n| **Criticality** | `.error`, `.warning`, `.success`, `.information` | Feedback states for forms and alerts |\n| **Emphasis** | `.muted`, `.branded` | Visual prominence adjustment |\n| **Anticipation** | `.constructive`, `.destructive`, `.caution` | Expected outcome of an action |\n\nAll families set the same channel variables. This uniformity means a component that supports `.error` automatically supports `.constructive`, `.branded`, and every other modifier — the component does not distinguish between them.\n\n## Why this matters for composition\n\nThe channel variable indirection is what makes modifiers compositional. Consider a form:\n\n\\`\\`\\`html\n<form class=\"error\">\n  <label>Email</label>\n  <input type=\"email\" />\n  <span class=\"helper-text\">Please enter a valid email</span>\n</form>\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nThe label, input, and helper text all read `--modifier-color-text`. None of them know about `.error`. If you replace `.error` with `.warning`, every descendant's color shifts. If you add a new component inside the form, it automatically participates in the modifier context as long as it reads the channel variable.\n\nThis compositionality breaks if a component references `--color-text-error` directly. That hard-codes the intent and prevents the component from working inside `.warning` or `.success` contexts. The contract is: **components reference channels, not intents**.\n\n## Nesting and override behavior\n\nModifiers can nest. An `.error` form might contain a `.success` badge:\n\n\\`\\`\\`html\n<form class=\"error\">\n  <div class=\"success\">\n    <span>Verified</span>\n  </div>\n</form>\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nThe inner `.success` sets `--modifier-color-text` to the success text color, overriding the `.error` value for its descendants. CSS custom property inheritance handles this automatically — the closest ancestor setting a channel variable wins.\n\nThis means modifiers compose without special logic: the last modifier in the DOM tree determines the visual output.\n\n## What modifiers do not do\n\nModifiers **do not** change layout, spacing, or typography. They only affect color-related channel variables. This is a deliberate constraint — color is the visual property most affected by context (error, success, brand emphasis), while layout is determined by the component's own specifications.\n\nModifiers also do not affect primitive tokens. A palette color like `--color-palette-red-600` is the same everywhere. Only semantic tokens participate in the modifier system, because only semantic tokens carry intent.\n\n## The cascade position\n\nModifier channel variables live in `@layer ds.modifiers`, which overrides `@layer ds.tokens` (base semantic values). This means a modifier can override a semantic token's value regardless of selector specificity, and a surface channel (in `@layer ds.surfaces`) can in turn override a modifier channel when appropriate.\n\nThe layered cascade ensures a predictable resolution order: tokens → modifiers → surfaces → states.\n\n## Common misconceptions\n\n### \"Modifiers change the theme\"\n\nModifiers do not change the theme. Theme switching (light/dark) changes _which values_ semantic tokens resolve to. Modifiers change _which semantic tokens_ a component reads. An `.error` modifier works identically in light mode and dark mode — the error text color is defined for both themes in the semantic token layer.\n\n### \"Components should detect the modifier\"\n\nComponents should not detect or respond to modifiers through JavaScript. The entire mechanism is CSS-only. A component that checks `classList.contains('error')` has broken the contract — it now depends on a specific modifier instead of reading the channel.\n\n### \"I need a new modifier for my use case\"\n\nNew modifiers are rarely needed. The existing families cover the standard intent space. If you need a novel color context, first check whether a combination of existing modifiers and surfaces achieves the result. If a new modifier family is genuinely needed, it should be added to `@canonical/styles` so that all components benefit from it.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- **Token Architecture**: why the system has three tiers\n- **Surface Depth Model**: how surfaces interact with modifiers\n- **How to support modifiers in a component**: practical steps for modifier integration\n- **Modifiers story**: live demonstration of modifier rebinding";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
